 Producer methods are `prepare(request)`, `owns(packet)`, `current(packet)` and `release(packet)`. Same-instance membership is an internal lifecycle check, not authentication. `current` rereads actual task/target/scope without recompiling context. It does not claim all memory sources remain current. A released/copied/foreign packet is refused. Local release deletes the packet receipt only; it cannot release a durable reservation. These checks provide cooperative currentness, not immunity to an arbitrary writer racing the final SQL call or native spawn.
 
-One producer has one outstanding I/O operation and bounded active receipts. Timeout stops waiting while retaining occupancy until the entire operation settles. Late results create no usable packet or later reads. A pending `current` operation likewise cannot return permission after release/timeout. The actual compiler now starts its four reads through safe Promise thunks and waits with `Promise.allSettled` before rethrowing any rejection. A failing query cannot release the outer producer slot while a sibling query remains unresolved. This bounds accumulation per producer instance, not across arbitrary independently created producers or native processes. The producer uses the monotonic process clock directly and introduces no injectable synchronous clock callback.
